{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "With fixed particle_mass, the implicit quantization condition maps (L, V0, n) to E_n through one shared mass scale, so width and depth dependence are the same for all samples apart from the existing finite-barrier structure.",
      "child_relation": "Release particle_mass as a sampled material/environment property so the level energy depends on the coupled combination sqrt(particle_mass*(E_n+V0))*L and on the induced dimensionless confinement strength particle_mass*V0*L**2/hbar**2, creating observable cross-condition changes in how E_n varies with L and V0.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "V0",
        "n",
        "particle_mass"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed L, V0, and n, heavier effective mass yields lower bound-state energies and can change whether a given level remains bound; equivalently, the slope of E_n versus L or V0 differs across masses, which a single refit with fixed mass cannot reproduce across mixed-material samples."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "eta",
        "expression": "particle_mass*V0*L**2/hbar**2",
        "role": "dimensionless confinement strength controlling the number and spacing of bound states"
      },
      {
        "name": "kL",
        "expression": "L*sqrt(2*particle_mass*(E_n + V0))/hbar",
        "role": "interior phase accumulation entering the quantization condition"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when samples span more than one material or effective-mass environment, with particle_mass varying enough that eta changes by order one while remaining in the bound-state regime E_n<0 and only retained levels are sampled.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, mixed masses already produce different E_n-versus-L or E_n-versus-V0 trends at the same n; in holdout, the same mass-dependent distortion persists at wider/deeper wells rather than collapsing to one universal fixed-mass curve.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law by fixing particle_mass to a single constant across all samples.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_input",
    "promoted_symbol": "particle_mass",
    "promotion_reason": "The realistic refinement is variation of effective mass across material realizations; this cannot be represented with only L, V0, and n when mass is held fixed, because the target mapping itself changes across samples rather than merely requiring coefficient refitting within one environment.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes one universal fixed mass, omitting material-dependent effective-mass variation that directly changes bound-state quantization and the retained set of admissible levels.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The particle has fixed mass and no spin-dependent interactions.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Different semiconductor or band-structure environments produce different effective masses, modifying both the interior wavelength and the finite-barrier penetration relation for the same geometric well.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "V0",
      "n",
      "particle_mass",
      "hbar"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: for one fixed particle_mass, E_n is determined by the finite-well boundary-matching condition as a function of width L, depth V0, and index n. The scientific gap is the fixed-environment assumption that the mass scale is identical for every sample. A natural successor is to treat particle_mass as a sampled input representing effective mass in different materials or subbands. Varied condition: particle_mass varies across samples while comparing the same or overlapping ranges of L, V0, and n; held fixed in the contrast: choose fixed L, V0, and n and compare E_n across masses, or fixed mass slices and compare the slope of E_n versus L. Qualitative contrast: the child predicts that heavier mass strengthens confinement, lowering E_n and altering the L- and V0-sensitivity through eta=particle_mass*V0*L**2/hbar**2. A parent with one shared fixed particle_mass cannot reproduce multiple such mass-conditioned trend families simultaneously; separate refits per mass would violate the single-parameter-set condition. This is a genuine target-mapping change, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the scored output E_n changes directly with the newly varied environment. The refinement is admissible when the experiment includes multiple effective-mass realizations and remains within the retained bound-level regime; if all data come from one material with effectively constant mass, then no increased complexity would be observable."
  },
